The configuration
gettingstarted-16-configuration
The page shows every parameter of sd.conf that SD knows. For each one: the value this SD is running with (what the CONFIG function reports), the value the file says now, the default, when a change takes effect, whether anything acts on the parameter, and whether this page can change it. A row marked * is one where the file and the running SD disagree, and the box at the foot says who will see the file's value. A name in the file that SD does not know is listed as unknown: SD will not start while the file holds it.
restart means a change takes effect when SD is next stopped and started (sd-solo -stop, then sd-solo -start): every session takes its settings from the running SD, not from the file. D shows only the rows where the file and SD disagree, S only the parameters the file sets. B shows the saved backup directory and R the operating system part of the settings report. F5 reads everything again.
E changes the parameter under the cursor when its Change column says e: it asks for a whole number, checks it against the range SD itself enforces, shows the exact command (set.config name number) and when the change takes effect, and runs it only when you answer Run. The verb then changes that one line of sd.conf in the Solo folder (when SD_CONFIG names another file, the page says so: set.config changes the Solo folder's only), keeps the file as it was beside it as sd.conf.bak, and the page reads the file and SD again to show what changed. Nothing runs elevated: the file is your own.
Only 14 tuning parameters can be changed here. For every other row the Change column says by hand, and E shows why (the doors, paths and commands, logs and diagnostics, sizes SD does not check, and parameters nothing acts on are not changed from SD) and the line to add to sd.conf yourself, with an editor. It is an administrator's page: type ADMIN at the prompt first.
